1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,400
[Night. Lamplight. A hand in a brown wool cuff taps a Morse key: one… two-two… three-three-three.]

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:05,430
[A sunlit kitchen. Kai yawns over a mug. Mira draws at the table. Biscuit sits very politely beside the toast.]

3
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,310
NARRATOR: I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

4
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:08,730
[Close on Mira. She puts her crayon down, picks up her drawing of the Sun and holds it up, proud.]

5
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:08,320
NARRATOR: I was drawing the Sun.

6
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:11,645
[Kai, sleepy, squints at her over his mug.]

7
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:11,525
NARRATOR: Who draws at six in the morning?

8
00:00:11,795 --> 00:00:13,645
[Mira, admiring her own work.]

9
00:00:11,895 --> 00:00:13,055
MIRA: Artists.

10
00:00:13,795 --> 00:00:16,400
[Close on Biscuit. His eyes slide slowly toward the toast on the edge of the table.]

11
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:16,280
NARRATOR: And Biscuit was planning a crime.

12
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:19,700
[Sydney, mid-afternoon. A surfer stands up on the wave of her life.]

13
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:19,835
NARRATOR: At that moment, half the world was in sunlight.

14
00:00:20,105 --> 00:00:21,955
[A paddy field before noon. A farmer straightens up and squints at the sky.]

15
00:00:22,105 --> 00:00:24,955
[A boy sprints for his school bus. The doors close on his nose and it pulls away without him.]

16
00:00:25,105 --> 00:00:26,955
[Back in the kitchen, Biscuit rises on his back legs and snatches the toast. Kai points, outraged.]

17
00:00:25,205 --> 00:00:26,595
NARRATOR: Biscuit, no!

18
00:00:27,105 --> 00:00:29,660
[Mid-snatch, the sunlight drains out of the room from the window. Everyone freezes in the blue dark. Biscuit still has the toast.]

19
00:00:27,205 --> 00:00:28,940
NARRATOR: Then the Sun went out.

20
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:30,760
[Sydney: the same wave, the same surfer, under sudden stars.]

21
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:31,860
[Tamil Nadu: the paddy goes black at noon.]

22
00:00:32,010 --> 00:00:32,960
[Manila: the boy at the empty bus stop, in the dark.]

23
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:36,160
[At the window: stars over the city at breakfast time. Mira folds her drawing into her pocket.]

24
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:34,650
MIRA: Is it night again?

25
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:39,460
[Kai turns from the window and puts his mug down on the table, without looking at it.]

26
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:38,295
NARRATOR: It's seven in the morning.

27
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:42,860
[Kai flicks the light switch. The kitchen lamp comes on, warm and ordinary. The window stays full of stars.]

28
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:44,070
NARRATOR: The lights still worked. The Sun didn't.

29
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:47,170
[Three hard knocks rattle the front door.]

30
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:47,050
NARRATOR: Then someone knocked. Three times.

31
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:50,470
[The door opens on Mr. Ito from upstairs: fully dressed, shoes on, a torch in one hand and a packed canvas bag in the other.]

32
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:51,335
MR. ITO: Good morning. Shoes on, please. Both of you.

33
00:00:51,605 --> 00:00:54,755
[Close on Ito, calm in the torchlight.]

34
00:00:51,705 --> 00:00:57,215
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito, from upstairs. He was seventy-three, and he was never in a hurry.

35
00:00:57,485 --> 00:01:00,635
[Close on the canvas bag in his hand: worn straps, a luggage tag, packed tight.]

36
00:00:57,585 --> 00:01:01,520
NARRATOR: His bag was already packed. It had been packed for years.

37
00:01:01,790 --> 00:01:04,940
[Ito nods past them at Biscuit, who still has the toast in his mouth.]

38
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:05,055
MR. ITO: And bring the dog. He has your toast.

39
00:01:05,325 --> 00:01:08,475
[Title: THE SUN WENT OUT.]

40
00:01:05,425 --> 00:01:07,915
NARRATOR: It took me a year to understand why.

41
00:01:08,985 --> 00:01:11,965
[Outside, the street is full. Pyjamas and work clothes, every phone held up to a starry sky.]

42
00:01:09,085 --> 00:01:11,845
NARRATOR: Outside, the whole street was looking up.

43
00:01:12,115 --> 00:01:13,965
[A man in a dressing gown shouts at the sky.]

44
00:01:12,215 --> 00:01:13,800
NARRATOR: It's just an eclipse!

45
00:01:14,115 --> 00:01:17,265
[Ito, unimpressed, to Mira.]

46
00:01:14,215 --> 00:01:18,380
MR. ITO: Eclipses are polite. They're over in seven minutes.

47
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:21,800
[On every phone in the street: a news channel, four live feeds. Tokyo crowds in the dark at 2:52 PM. Cairo. A grounded airport. New York, asleep.]

48
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:21,835
NARRATOR: It was happening everywhere, at the same moment.

49
00:01:22,405 --> 00:01:25,510
[The anchor, and beside her a feed of New York at night: dark windows, a city asleep.]

50
00:01:22,505 --> 00:01:25,090
NARRATOR: In New York, it was two in the morning.

51
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:28,810
[The feeds again, hours later: alarm clocks ringing in black bedrooms, crowds in the streets.]

52
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:29,045
NARRATOR: Most of them woke up to a morning that never came.

53
00:01:29,715 --> 00:01:32,865
[Ito turns to Kai and nods up at the roof of their building.]

54
00:01:29,815 --> 00:01:33,455
MR. ITO: Up to the roof. Kai, bring my telescope.

55
00:01:33,725 --> 00:01:36,875
[The stairwell, sixth floor. Ito climbs ahead with the torch. Kai hauls the heavy telescope case, puffing. Mira skips behind.]

56
00:01:33,825 --> 00:01:38,010
NARRATOR: Kai carried it up six floors. He complained about every one.

57
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:41,430
[The roof. Ito sets his bag down; Kai drops the case with a groan. Ito looks east, where the Sun rose an hour ago.]

58
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:42,640
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito came up here every morning to watch the sunrise.

59
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:46,060
[He sets the torch on the ground and builds the brass telescope with quick, sure hands.]

60
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:45,545
NARRATOR: That morning, he had seen the last one.

61
00:01:46,195 --> 00:01:47,960
MR. ITO: Venus first.

62
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:50,265
[Low in the east, one bright point still shining: Venus.]

63
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:50,145
MIRA: Why is that one still on?

64
00:01:50,415 --> 00:01:53,565
[Diagram: the Sun as a dark question mark, Venus catching its light and passing it on to Earth.]

65
00:01:50,515 --> 00:01:56,905
MR. ITO: Venus has no light of its own. It's the Sun's light, coming the long way round.

66
00:01:57,175 --> 00:02:00,325
[Ito, eyes on his watch.]

67
00:01:57,275 --> 00:02:01,815
MR. ITO: If it happened at the Sun, Venus goes out at seven-oh-four.

68
00:02:02,085 --> 00:02:04,695
[They sit on the roof and wait. Kai checks his watch: 7:03.]

69
00:02:02,185 --> 00:02:03,575
NARRATOR: So we waited.

70
00:02:04,845 --> 00:02:07,095
[At four minutes past seven, Venus flickers and goes out.]

71
00:02:07,245 --> 00:02:10,395
[Ito, very quietly.]

72
00:02:07,345 --> 00:02:09,935
MR. ITO: Then it happened at the Sun.

73
00:02:10,605 --> 00:02:13,755
[Diagram: Jupiter, far out. A long light path. A clock.]

74
00:02:10,705 --> 00:02:14,245
MR. ITO: Jupiter is further out. Eight-oh-four.

75
00:02:14,515 --> 00:02:17,600
[Jupiter, centred in the telescope crosshairs, winks out.]

76
00:02:14,615 --> 00:02:17,080
NARRATOR: At four minutes past eight, it did.

77
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:20,900
[Ito taps the telescope three times, the way he always does.]

78
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:23,065
NARRATOR: He tapped the telescope three times. He always did that. I never asked why.

79
00:02:23,335 --> 00:02:26,185
[A small dome on a dark mountain. Beside it a radio dish turns toward one bright star.]

80
00:02:26,335 --> 00:02:28,985
[A small observatory at night. A young man with round glasses bends over a light table.]

81
00:02:29,135 --> 00:02:31,885
[An old eclipse plate under a magnifier. One faint line through the corona, far too straight. A red pen circles it.]

82
00:02:32,035 --> 00:02:35,185
[Ito’s battery radio on the kitchen table.]

83
00:02:32,135 --> 00:02:36,750
(RADIO): Phone networks are down across the city. Please stay indoors.

84
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:39,400
[Kai, already pulling on his shoes.]

85
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,280
NARRATOR: Everyone's going to the shops.

86
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:42,700
[Ito takes a folded list out of his bag and hands it to Kai.]

87
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:42,540
MR. ITO: Then so are we.

88
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:46,100
[The whole street runs the same way, in summer clothes under streetlights: toward the supermarket.]

89
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:46,515
NARRATOR: Kai was right. The whole city had the same idea.

90
00:02:46,785 --> 00:02:49,520
[Half-empty shelves. Kai steers a trolley, reading Ito’s list.]

91
00:02:46,885 --> 00:02:49,400
NARRATOR: Candles. Rice. Batteries. Water.

92
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:51,655
[Mira marches back with an armful of dog treats. Biscuit loses his mind.]

93
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:51,535
MIRA: They're on Biscuit's list.

94
00:02:51,805 --> 00:02:54,955
[Two grown men in a tug-of-war over the last electric heater.]

95
00:02:51,905 --> 00:02:55,415
NARRATOR: Two grown men fought over the last electric heater.

96
00:02:55,685 --> 00:02:58,835
[Ito watches the fight, deadpan.]

97
00:02:55,785 --> 00:02:59,250
MR. ITO: Heaters need electricity. They'll learn.

98
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,080
[A hospital corridor turned shelter. At the nurses’ station, a nurse in scrubs holds the mic of an old ham radio.]

99
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:01,960
NARRATOR: Kai? It’s Mum. Can you hear me?

100
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:04,715
[Their flat, that evening. Mr. Ito’s ham radio crackles on the desk. Kai grabs the mic.]

101
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,595
NARRATOR: Mum! Are you okay?

102
00:03:04,865 --> 00:03:08,015
[Mum, tired, smiling at the sound of him.]

103
00:03:04,965 --> 00:03:08,305
NARRATOR: I'm fine, love. But I can't come home yet.

104
00:03:08,575 --> 00:03:11,725
[Wide: every bed full, cots lining the corridor, people under blankets as far as the doors.]

105
00:03:08,675 --> 00:03:12,415
NARRATOR: The whole city is coming here. They need every nurse.

106
00:03:12,685 --> 00:03:14,535
[Mira leans in to the mic in Kai’s hand.]

107
00:03:12,785 --> 00:03:14,400
MIRA: When are you coming home?

108
00:03:14,685 --> 00:03:17,835
[Mum closes her eyes for a second, then makes her voice bright.]

109
00:03:14,785 --> 00:03:19,150
NARRATOR: Soon. I'll call every night at seven. Stay with Mr. Ito.

110
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:22,570
[Kai hangs the mic back on the radio. Ito, by the window, watching them.]

111
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,005
NARRATOR: She called every night at seven. We never missed one.

112
00:03:23,275 --> 00:03:25,560
[Later. Kai and Mira on the rug. Kai finally asks it.]

113
00:03:23,375 --> 00:03:25,140
NARRATOR: Is the Sun… dead?

114
00:03:25,710 --> 00:03:28,860
[Diagram: the Moon tugs a bulge of ocean toward it. The hidden Sun tugs too.]

115
00:03:25,810 --> 00:03:29,775
MR. ITO: If the Sun were gone, its pull would be gone too.

116
00:03:30,045 --> 00:03:33,195
[The Sun’s bulge is removed: the tide shrinks by a third.]

117
00:03:30,145 --> 00:03:33,935
MR. ITO: And tomorrow's tide would be a third smaller.

118
00:03:34,205 --> 00:03:37,355
[Ito takes an old tide table out of his bag and hands it to Kai.]

119
00:03:35,055 --> 00:03:38,470
MR. ITO: Go and look. High tide is at eleven.

120
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:41,890
[Day 1. The harbour. Torchlight on boats and black water.]

121
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:42,505
NARRATOR: So the next morning, Kai and I walked down to the harbour.

122
00:03:42,775 --> 00:03:44,660
[The torch finds the painted tide gauge. The water sits exactly on Ito’s pencilled mark.]

123
00:03:42,875 --> 00:03:44,540
MIRA: It's right on the line!

124
00:03:44,810 --> 00:03:46,990
[Kai stares at the tide table, then up.]

125
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:46,870
NARRATOR: So the Sun's still there.

126
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:49,070
[Mira looks up at the black sky.]

127
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:48,650
MIRA: Then where is it?

128
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:52,370
[The two of them, tiny at the edge of the freezing harbour, staring at where the Sun should be.]

129
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:53,180
NARRATOR: The Sun hadn't gone anywhere. Something was in front of it.

130
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:57,100
[Mira’s mitten points down through a thin skin of new ice: under it, a fish glides past in the torch beam.]

131
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:57,215
MIRA: The sea's growing a skin. The fish don't mind!

132
00:03:57,485 --> 00:04:00,635
[Day 2. A press conference. Behind the scientist, an infrared image: a warm ring where the Sun should be.]

133
00:03:57,585 --> 00:04:01,175
NEWS ANCHOR: Infrared telescopes have found something around the Sun.

134
00:04:01,445 --> 00:04:04,595
[The scientist at the podium, the warm ring behind her.]

135
00:04:01,545 --> 00:04:05,560
NARRATOR: It's warm. It's round. And it's twice as wide as the Sun.

136
00:04:06,130 --> 00:04:08,485
[Pull back from where the Sun should be: a colossal sphere of dark slats wrapped around the star, faintly glowing at the seams.]

137
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:08,365
NARRATOR: There was something around the Sun.

138
00:04:08,635 --> 00:04:11,785
[The camera glides around the shell. Each of its slats is wider than Earth.]

139
00:04:08,735 --> 00:04:11,050
NARRATOR: A shell. Wrapped around a star.

140
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:15,270
[The feeds: candlelit prayer in Rome, protests in London, radar rooms, a stampede for rice in Mumbai.]

141
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:15,360
NEWS ANCHOR: Governments are asking everyone to stay calm.

142
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:17,350
NARRATOR: Nobody was calm.

143
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:20,350
[Their flat. The news on the little TV. Mira turns from it to Mr. Ito.]

144
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:20,230
MIRA: Mr. Ito, who built it?

145
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:23,650
[Ito crosses to the TV and switches it off. He does not look at her.]

146
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:24,165
MR. ITO: It's bedtime, Mira.

147
00:04:24,435 --> 00:04:27,585
[Mira, in the dark. The clock says four in the afternoon.]

148
00:04:24,535 --> 00:04:26,620
NARRATOR: It was four in the afternoon.

149
00:04:27,270 --> 00:04:29,760
NARRATOR: He'd never sent me to bed before.

150
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:33,580
[The whole sky ignites: green and violet curtains over the frozen city.]

151
00:04:30,530 --> 00:04:35,470
(RADIO): …a solar storm that left the Sun four days ago, arriving tonight.

152
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:38,225
[Mira, face lit green, watching.]

153
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:37,505
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

154
00:04:38,375 --> 00:04:41,225
[The young astronomer studies three plates pinned to the wall beside a star chart.]

155
00:04:41,375 --> 00:04:44,425
[Red lines, extended from each plate, meet at one faint star on the chart. He writes 21 beside it.]

156
00:04:44,575 --> 00:04:47,505
[The city from above. Districts go dark one after another, rolling toward their street.]

157
00:04:44,675 --> 00:04:47,385
NARRATOR: On Day 7, the power went out.

158
00:04:47,655 --> 00:04:50,805
[Earth’s night side from orbit: the lights of whole countries fading out.]

159
00:04:47,755 --> 00:04:51,540
NARRATOR: Not just ours. All over the world, the grids gave out.

160
00:04:51,810 --> 00:04:54,960
[Blankets taped over the windows of one room. Mira holds the corner while Kai tapes.]

161
00:04:51,910 --> 00:04:55,320
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito showed us how to make one warm room.

162
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:58,740
[Kai carries the milk from the dead freezer out onto the frosted balcony and lines it up with the rest.]

163
00:04:55,690 --> 00:04:58,275
NARRATOR: Free fridge. Finally, some good news.

164
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:02,140
[Kai strikes a match, lights the candles, shakes it out.]

165
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:02,075
NARRATOR: That night, Kai pretended he wasn't scared.

166
00:05:02,345 --> 00:05:05,480
[Close in candlelight: Kai’s hand clamped round Mira’s mitten, knuckles pale.]

167
00:05:02,445 --> 00:05:04,860
NARRATOR: He was holding my hand too hard.

168
00:05:05,630 --> 00:05:07,590
[Torch beam in the stairwell. Grey smoke curls out from under a neighbour’s door.]

169
00:05:05,730 --> 00:05:07,470
NARRATOR: Then we smelled smoke.

170
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,890
[The torch finds door 3B: firelight flickering underneath, smoke pouring out.]

171
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:11,055
MR. ITO: They're burning furniture. Indoors.

172
00:05:11,325 --> 00:05:14,475
[Ito, in the smoke, suddenly very loud.]

173
00:05:11,425 --> 00:05:14,965
MR. ITO: Everyone out! It's the gas you can't see.

174
00:05:15,235 --> 00:05:18,040
[Down the stairs: neighbours coughing into their sleeves, Kai with the torch, Mira at his heels, Ito banging on doors, Biscuit ahead.]

175
00:05:15,335 --> 00:05:17,920
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

176
00:05:18,190 --> 00:05:20,970
[The street door bangs open into a wall of wind and snow. Biscuit bolts into the dark. Kai lunges, torch swinging.]

177
00:05:18,290 --> 00:05:20,850
NARRATOR: At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

178
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:23,475
[Mira screams his name into the storm. Nothing comes back.]

179
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:22,355
MIRA: BISCUIT!

180
00:05:23,625 --> 00:05:26,775
[Day 8, seven o’clock. Mum at the hospital radio, in the same scrubs, more tired.]

181
00:05:23,725 --> 00:05:27,490
NARRATOR: Mira, listen to me. Biscuit is a clever dog.

182
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:30,910
[In the warm room, Mira clutches the mic, listening.]

183
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:30,625
NARRATOR: Clever dogs find warm places.

184
00:05:31,295 --> 00:05:34,445
[Mira turns to Kai.]

185
00:05:31,395 --> 00:05:33,485
MIRA: Kai. What’s warm out there?

186
00:05:33,835 --> 00:05:35,620
NARRATOR: …The metro. The vents.

187
00:05:36,190 --> 00:05:38,075
[Kai walks the frozen street alone with a torch. Buried cars. Silent snow.]

188
00:05:36,290 --> 00:05:37,955
NARRATOR: Biscuit! Come on, boy!

189
00:05:38,225 --> 00:05:41,375
[Kai turns on the spot, sweeping the torch. Nothing.]

190
00:05:38,325 --> 00:05:42,465
NARRATOR: Kai searched all night. Then again, it was always night now.

191
00:05:42,735 --> 00:05:44,785
[Somewhere far off, a single bark (a little "WOOF!" pops out of the dark). Kai’s head snaps round.]

192
00:05:44,935 --> 00:05:47,240
[The torch beam finds a line of fresh paw prints in the snow.]

193
00:05:45,035 --> 00:05:47,120
NARRATOR: Paw prints. Fresh ones.

194
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:50,540
[The prints lead across the street to a cloud of steam rising from a metro entrance.]

195
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:50,475
NARRATOR: They led to the only warm thing on the street.

196
00:05:50,745 --> 00:05:53,895
[Biscuit is curled against the warm grate, frost on his whiskers. He sees Kai. His tail explodes. Kai drops the torch in the snow and grabs him.]

197
00:05:52,595 --> 00:05:54,760
NARRATOR: You stupid, brilliant dog.

198
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:58,180
[Cut-away of the ground: frozen surface, then the metro tunnels, then warm rock below.]

199
00:05:55,130 --> 00:05:59,190
NARRATOR: A few metres down, the ground stays around ten degrees.

200
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:02,610
[Kai picks up his torch. Biscuit trots toward the metro stairs and looks back. Come on.]

201
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:03,120
NARRATOR: Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd found the way down.

202
00:06:03,390 --> 00:06:06,540
[Down the metro stairs: Kai carrying Mira on his back, Ito with his bag, and behind them a line of torches.]

203
00:06:03,490 --> 00:06:07,075
NARRATOR: By morning, half the street had followed him underground.

204
00:06:07,345 --> 00:06:10,495
[Feeds from around the world: tents in metro halls, families on escalators, shelters cut into rock.]

205
00:06:07,445 --> 00:06:10,755
NARRATOR: All over the world, cities moved underground.

206
00:06:11,025 --> 00:06:14,175
[Day 30. The metro is a town: tents on the platforms, string lights, laundry lines.]

207
00:06:11,125 --> 00:06:14,910
NARRATOR: By Day 30, Harbour Street station was a town.

208
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:18,330
[A disused tunnel full of grow lights and lettuce. A chalk menu: lettuce, lettuce, lettuce. A small kid glares at yet another lettuce.]

209
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:20,015
NARRATOR: We grew food under pink lamps. Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

210
00:06:20,285 --> 00:06:23,435
[Seven a.m. The big lamps in the station roof warm up from orange to gold.]

211
00:06:20,385 --> 00:06:24,770
NARRATOR: At seven every morning, the engineers turned on the big lamps.

212
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:27,400
[Faces turned up into the gold light. Mira jumps. Ito smiles.]

213
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:27,280
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

214
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:30,700
[In a parked carriage turned classroom, Mira (the tallest child in the room) shows four much smaller kids her drawing from the last morning.]

215
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:32,060
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And it's warm on your face.

216
00:06:32,330 --> 00:06:35,480
[A small kid holds up her own drawing: a grey sun.]

217
00:06:32,430 --> 00:06:35,715
NARRATOR: Some of them had already forgotten what colour it was.

218
00:06:36,585 --> 00:06:39,735
[Mira looks at the grey sun, then folds her own drawing carefully back into her pocket.]

219
00:06:36,685 --> 00:06:39,350
NARRATOR: I kept mine in my pocket, all year.

220
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:43,170
[Biscuit, noticeably rounder, receives snacks from a ring of adoring kids.]

221
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:43,285
NARRATOR: Biscuit got a fan club. And a bigger belly.

222
00:06:43,555 --> 00:06:46,405
[A typed letter under the lamp: the Council cannot support further observations, nor any transmission. His thumb covers the name.]

223
00:06:46,555 --> 00:06:49,605
[The receiver at his ear. Down the line, a colleague laughs. He says nothing.]

224
00:06:49,755 --> 00:06:52,210
[Day 40. Ito’s workbench in the station, his radio and his bag on it. Mira asks the question he would not answer.]

225
00:06:49,855 --> 00:06:52,090
MIRA: Mr. Ito. Who built it?

226
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:55,340
[Kai, backing her up.]

227
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:55,220
NARRATOR: Nobody builds a thing that size in a day.

228
00:06:55,490 --> 00:06:58,640
[Ito turns to the bag on his bench and takes out an old photograph.]

229
00:06:55,590 --> 00:06:57,705
MR. ITO: No. Nobody did.

230
00:06:59,090 --> 00:07:02,240
[An old eclipse photograph in Ito’s hand: three faint, perfectly straight lines crossing the corona.]

231
00:06:59,190 --> 00:07:03,830
MR. ITO: Nineteen-nineteen. A total eclipse. Look at the lines.

232
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:07,250
[A window blind with its slats open, sunlight streaming through. Beside it, the Sun in a ring of the same slats, edge-on, light pouring between them.]

233
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:08,765
MR. ITO: It was always there. Edge-on, like the slats of a blind.

234
00:07:09,035 --> 00:07:12,185
[Every slat turns at once. The window goes dark. The Sun’s light is trapped inside.]

235
00:07:09,135 --> 00:07:13,275
MR. ITO: On the morning the Sun went out, every slat turned.

236
00:07:13,945 --> 00:07:16,280
[Mira, sharp.]

237
00:07:14,045 --> 00:07:16,160
MIRA: You've seen those lines before.

238
00:07:16,430 --> 00:07:19,580
[Ito, not meeting her eyes. He puts the photograph back in the bag.]

239
00:07:16,530 --> 00:07:20,270
MR. ITO: Once. A long time ago. Nobody listened.

240
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:24,090
[The two of them in the lamplight. Mira watches his back.]

241
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:25,000
NARRATOR: I believed him. It was true. It just wasn’t all of it.

242
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:29,020
[Day 100. The lamps flicker, buzz, and die. Darkness, then a hundred torches clicking on.]

243
00:07:25,970 --> 00:07:29,335
NARRATOR: On Day 100, the generators failed.

244
00:07:29,605 --> 00:07:32,755
[Ito follows the engineers into the dark tunnel, torch in hand.]

245
00:07:29,705 --> 00:07:33,070
MR. ITO: I know a little about old machines.

246
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:36,450
[The hospital by lantern light, the generators failing there too. Mum at the radio.]

247
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:36,330
NARRATOR: Mira? Our generators are going too.

248
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:39,635
[The workshop by candlelight. Mira holds the mic in both hands.]

249
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:39,315
NARRATOR: If you can't hear me for a while, don't—

250
00:07:39,785 --> 00:07:42,935
[Static. Nothing. Mira sets the mic down very carefully.]

251
00:07:39,885 --> 00:07:41,400
MIRA: Mum? Mum!

252
00:07:43,385 --> 00:07:45,565
[Under every blanket they own, Kai and Mira shiver. Breath smoking even down here.]

253
00:07:43,485 --> 00:07:45,445
NARRATOR: That was the coldest night.

254
00:07:45,715 --> 00:07:48,800
[Kai’s face in the last candle light. For once, no joke.]

255
00:07:45,815 --> 00:07:47,880
NARRATOR: How does this end, Mira?

256
00:07:48,950 --> 00:07:51,785
[Mira, small and certain.]

257
00:07:49,050 --> 00:07:51,065
MIRA: With the lights coming back on.

258
00:07:51,935 --> 00:07:55,085
[Day 180. The lamps blaze back on. The whole station roars.]

259
00:07:52,035 --> 00:07:57,470
NARRATOR: On Day 180, Mr. Ito and the engineers got them running again.

260
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:00,890
[The hospital, dark and freezing. Mum, alive, at the radio, her breath smoking.]

261
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,805
NARRATOR: Kai, Mira, it’s Mum. Our power’s gone. Yours is back.

262
00:08:02,075 --> 00:08:05,225
[The workshop, lit again. Kai holds the mic. Mira’s hands fly to her mouth.]

263
00:08:02,175 --> 00:08:05,690
NARRATOR: So we're bringing everyone to you. Across the river.

264
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:08,890
[In the workshop, Kai has the mic. Mira is laughing and crying.]

265
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:08,770
NARRATOR: Mum, it's sixty below out there.

266
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:11,950
[Mum, grinning into the mic.]

267
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:11,430
NARRATOR: Then we'd better walk fast.

268
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:15,250
[Night. The frozen river under a sky full of stars. A line of lanterns crossing the ice from the hospital: Mum leading nurses and patients.]

269
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:17,510
NARRATOR: She walked two kilometres across the ice at sixty below, with forty patients.

270
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:20,930
[A woman in a navy coat over nurse’s scrubs walks out of the tunnel with a lantern. She sets it down. Mira and Kai run.]

271
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:19,645
NARRATOR: And then she was home.

272
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:23,290
[Mum turns to Ito, grease-stained and grinning.]

273
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:23,170
NARRATOR: You kept them alive.

274
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:26,050
[Ito, quietly.]

275
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:25,530
MR. ITO: They kept me alive.

276
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:28,050
[Later. Ito at his bench with wires and a little dish. Mira leans in.]

277
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:27,760
MIRA: What are you building?

278
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:31,035
[Ito, holding it up, eyes on the ceiling as if he can see through it.]

279
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:30,515
MR. ITO: Something to listen with.

280
00:08:31,185 --> 00:08:34,335
[Day 300. The station’s big screen: a beam traced up from the Sun to one star in Cassiopeia, ringed.]

281
00:08:31,285 --> 00:08:34,700
NEWS ANCHOR: Astronomers have found a beam of light leaving the shell.

282
00:08:34,970 --> 00:08:38,120
[The station crowd, from behind, watching the big screen over the platform. Ito slowly turns around.]

283
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:42,335
NEWS ANCHOR: It's aimed at a star called HD 219134. Twenty-one light-years away.

284
00:08:43,005 --> 00:08:45,740
[In the crowd under the screen, Ito goes white. Barely a whisper.]

285
00:08:43,105 --> 00:08:44,620
MR. ITO: Twenty-one.

286
00:08:45,890 --> 00:08:48,320
[Kai and Mira beside him. Mira watches Ito. He says nothing more.]

287
00:08:45,990 --> 00:08:47,900
NARRATOR: I didn't ask what it meant.

288
00:08:48,470 --> 00:08:51,620
[The shell from far away, a needle of light leaving it toward one faint star.]

289
00:08:48,570 --> 00:08:51,385
NARRATOR: All the Sun's light, going to one star.

290
00:08:52,255 --> 00:08:54,905
[At the transmitter. His finger hovers over the key. He looks at the chart: 21.]

291
00:08:55,055 --> 00:08:56,505
[He presses it, once.]

292
00:08:56,655 --> 00:08:59,805
[Day 365, 6:50 in the morning. All of them on the surface, tiny under the brightest Milky Way anyone has ever seen.]

293
00:08:56,755 --> 00:09:02,765
NARRATOR: On Day 365, we all went up to the surface. One year, to the minute.

294
00:09:03,035 --> 00:09:06,185
[Mira grins up at Kai.]

295
00:09:03,135 --> 00:09:06,649
MIRA: One whole lap around the Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

296
00:09:06,919 --> 00:09:10,069
[Ito’s little receiver dish clicks in his hands. His face changes.]

297
00:09:07,019 --> 00:09:10,934
MR. ITO: The beam's stopped. Something's coming from the Sun.

298
00:09:11,204 --> 00:09:14,354
[Pulses: one, then two, then three. Kai counts along.]

299
00:09:11,304 --> 00:09:12,089
NARRATOR: One.

300
00:09:12,839 --> 00:09:13,374
NARRATOR: Two.

301
00:09:14,124 --> 00:09:14,859
NARRATOR: Three.

302
00:09:15,829 --> 00:09:18,239
[The receiver’s screen. The counter passes three and keeps climbing, faster and faster.]

303
00:09:15,929 --> 00:09:17,619
NARRATOR: Then it didn't stop.

304
00:09:18,389 --> 00:09:20,969
[Kai stops counting.]

305
00:09:18,489 --> 00:09:20,849
NARRATOR: That's not three. That's hundreds.

306
00:09:21,119 --> 00:09:24,269
[Ito, reading the counter as it stops.]

307
00:09:21,219 --> 00:09:25,009
MR. ITO: One thousand, six hundred and seventy-nine.

308
00:09:25,579 --> 00:09:27,534
[Kai, deadpan.]

309
00:09:25,679 --> 00:09:27,214
NARRATOR: Is that a lot?

310
00:09:27,684 --> 00:09:30,834
[Ito, not looking up.]

311
00:09:27,784 --> 00:09:31,974
MR. ITO: It's twenty-three times seventy-three. Nothing else.

312
00:09:32,544 --> 00:09:35,694
[Mira’s eyes go wide. She is the artist.]

313
00:09:32,644 --> 00:09:34,634
MIRA: Like squares on graph paper.

314
00:09:35,284 --> 00:09:36,624
MIRA: It's a picture!

315
00:09:37,394 --> 00:09:40,544
[The pulses fall into a grid, 23 across and 73 down, filling row by row.]

316
00:09:37,494 --> 00:09:41,034
MR. ITO: Twenty-three across. Seventy-three down.

317
00:09:41,394 --> 00:09:44,544
[The finished picture, framed at the top: rows of dots counting one to ten.]

318
00:09:41,494 --> 00:09:45,034
NARRATOR: At the top, they'd finished his count. Up to ten.

319
00:09:45,304 --> 00:09:48,454
[Lower: a tall figure with a crest and three-fingered hands. Beside it, small, a human.]

320
00:09:45,404 --> 00:09:48,639
NARRATOR: Then them. And us, beside them, very small.

321
00:09:49,309 --> 00:09:52,459
[Lower still: their star with its planets, one raised; the beam; our Sun inside its shell; Earth.]

322
00:09:49,409 --> 00:09:53,419
NARRATOR: Their star. Their home. And our Sun, inside its shell.

323
00:09:54,289 --> 00:09:56,644
[Mira turns to Ito. He is staring at the little screen.]

324
00:09:54,389 --> 00:09:56,524
MIRA: Mr. Ito? What is it?

325
00:09:56,794 --> 00:09:59,129
[Ito, quietly.]

326
00:09:56,894 --> 00:09:58,609
MR. ITO: It's a reply.

327
00:09:59,279 --> 00:10:01,129
[Mira, not understanding.]

328
00:09:59,379 --> 00:10:00,394
MIRA: To who?

329
00:10:01,279 --> 00:10:03,789
[Ito, finally.]

330
00:10:01,379 --> 00:10:02,669
MR. ITO: To me.

331
00:10:03,939 --> 00:10:07,089
[The Morse key again. One… two-two… three-three-three: the same rhythm as the pulses.]

332
00:10:07,539 --> 00:10:10,689
[He walks out of the dome. Over the far ridge, the sky goes gold, and the Sun rises.]

333
00:10:12,139 --> 00:10:14,189
[Close: his hand on the dome railing in the sunrise. Tap. Tap. Tap.]

334
00:10:14,339 --> 00:10:17,189
[His face in the sunrise, round glasses glinting.]

335
00:10:17,339 --> 00:10:20,489
[Match cut: the same face, forty-three years older, in starlight, tapping his receiver three times.]

336
00:10:17,439 --> 00:10:22,354
NARRATOR: The man in the observatory was Mr. Ito. Forty-three years ago.

337
00:10:23,024 --> 00:10:26,174
[The star chart again: the three lines meeting at one star. 21.]

338
00:10:23,124 --> 00:10:26,339
NARRATOR: He found the lines, and the star they pointed to.

339
00:10:26,609 --> 00:10:29,759
[His young hand on the key.]

340
00:10:26,709 --> 00:10:30,949
NARRATOR: They told him not to. He knocked anyway. One. Two. Three.

341
00:10:31,619 --> 00:10:34,769
[Earth and a star 21 light-years apart. The knock crosses out: 1983 → 2004. The answer crosses back: 2004 → 2026.]

342
00:10:31,719 --> 00:10:35,809
NARRATOR: Twenty-one years to get there. Twenty-one years to come back.

343
00:10:36,079 --> 00:10:39,229
[Ito turns to Mira and tells her, plainly.]

344
00:10:36,179 --> 00:10:39,819
MR. ITO: It's a telephone, Mira. Theirs. I dialled it.

345
00:10:40,089 --> 00:10:43,199
[Ito looks up at the dark where the Sun is.]

346
00:10:40,189 --> 00:10:42,679
MR. ITO: And now they've answered.

347
00:10:43,349 --> 00:10:46,499
[The shell and its beam, the Sun’s whole output squeezed into one thin line across the dark.]

348
00:10:43,449 --> 00:10:48,789
MR. ITO: To call that far, it needed the Sun. All of it. For one whole year.

349
00:10:49,059 --> 00:10:52,209
[Mira and Ito in the starlight.]

350
00:10:49,159 --> 00:10:52,824
MR. ITO: It sent them everything it ever heard from us.

351
00:10:53,174 --> 00:10:54,309
MIRA: Like what?

352
00:10:54,779 --> 00:10:57,929
[Ito, almost smiling.]

353
00:10:54,879 --> 00:10:59,219
MR. ITO: Every song. Every voice. A hundred years of us.

354
00:10:59,889 --> 00:11:02,344
[Mira asks the hardest question.]

355
00:10:59,989 --> 00:11:01,824
MIRA: Did they know it would hurt us?

356
00:11:02,494 --> 00:11:05,029
[Ito has no answer.]

357
00:11:02,594 --> 00:11:04,109
MR. ITO: I don't know.

358
00:11:05,179 --> 00:11:08,329
[Mira steps in and takes his arm.]

359
00:11:05,279 --> 00:11:07,444
MR. ITO: I'm sorry, Mira.

360
00:11:08,714 --> 00:11:11,864
[The picture again. The camera slides to the bottom: an empty square, pulsing.]

361
00:11:08,814 --> 00:11:12,149
NARRATOR: At the bottom of their picture was an empty square.

362
00:11:12,819 --> 00:11:14,979
[Mira, very still.]

363
00:11:12,919 --> 00:11:14,259
MIRA: It's our turn.

364
00:11:15,129 --> 00:11:18,279
[Ito looks up at the black sky where the Sun is.]

365
00:11:15,229 --> 00:11:20,044
MR. ITO: It's stopped sending. But it's still shut. It's waiting for us.

366
00:11:20,314 --> 00:11:23,464
[Day 367. The news: an emergency session, a hundred flags, and no answer.]

367
00:11:20,414 --> 00:11:23,779
NEWS ANCHOR: World leaders still cannot agree on what to say.

368
00:11:24,049 --> 00:11:27,199
[Feeds from capitals: crowds outside parliaments, placards, arguments.]

369
00:11:24,149 --> 00:11:26,909
NARRATOR: Two days later, they were still arguing.

370
00:11:27,479 --> 00:11:30,629
[The workshop. Ito, Kai and Mira, the little receiver dish on the bench.]

371
00:11:27,579 --> 00:11:30,994
MR. ITO: Every day we wait, the Sun stays shut.

372
00:11:31,264 --> 00:11:33,619
[Kai, out of ideas.]

373
00:11:31,364 --> 00:11:33,199
NARRATOR: So what do we even send?

374
00:11:33,769 --> 00:11:36,549
[Mira takes the folded drawing out of her pocket and holds it up.]

375
00:11:34,919 --> 00:11:36,429
MIRA: Can we send this?

376
00:11:36,699 --> 00:11:39,534
[Ito looks at the drawing for a long moment.]

377
00:11:36,799 --> 00:11:39,014
MR. ITO: Yes. I think we can.

378
00:11:39,684 --> 00:11:42,834
[Mira’s crayon sun under Ito’s lamp, then the same picture as squares: a sun with rays, a little girl standing under it.]

379
00:11:39,784 --> 00:11:42,244
NARRATOR: My drawing from the morning it went out.

380
00:11:42,594 --> 00:11:46,154
NARRATOR: A big yellow sun. And me, standing under it.

381
00:11:46,924 --> 00:11:50,074
[Kai, suddenly very sixteen. Ito, perfectly calm.]

382
00:11:47,024 --> 00:11:49,309
NARRATOR: Are we even allowed to do this?

383
00:11:49,659 --> 00:11:51,374
MR. ITO: Probably not.

384
00:11:52,244 --> 00:11:55,394
[Up the frozen metro stairs: Kai hauls the big transmitter dish on his back, step by step, Mira behind him with the drawing.]

385
00:11:52,344 --> 00:11:57,029
NARRATOR: Kai carried the transmitter up to the surface. He didn’t complain once.

386
00:11:57,299 --> 00:12:00,449
[6:35 in the morning, on the surface. The transmitter on its tripod, aimed at the hidden Sun. Ito at the key.]

387
00:11:57,399 --> 00:12:03,014
MR. ITO: If we send it at 6:35, the light gets back at 6:52.

388
00:12:03,284 --> 00:12:05,564
[Mira, holding her drawing.]

389
00:12:03,384 --> 00:12:05,144
MIRA: The same minute it went out.

390
00:12:05,714 --> 00:12:08,864
[Ito presses the key. The transmitter light goes red.]

391
00:12:05,814 --> 00:12:09,749
NARRATOR: He pressed the key, the way he had in 1983.

392
00:12:10,019 --> 00:12:13,169
[Ito straightens up, looking east.]

393
00:12:10,119 --> 00:12:13,409
MR. ITO: Eight minutes to get there. Eight to come back.

394
00:12:13,679 --> 00:12:16,784
[Wide. Five small figures in the snow, the transmitter glowing. A clock: 16:40.]

395
00:12:13,779 --> 00:12:16,264
NARRATOR: Sixteen minutes and forty seconds.

396
00:12:16,934 --> 00:12:20,084
[All over the world, people on rooftops in the dark, looking up at the same patch of sky.]

397
00:12:17,034 --> 00:12:20,649
NARRATOR: The whole world was looking at the same patch of sky.

398
00:12:20,919 --> 00:12:24,069
[Mira, scared. Mum pulls her in.]

399
00:12:21,019 --> 00:12:22,534
MIRA: What if it doesn’t work?

400
00:12:22,884 --> 00:12:24,849
NARRATOR: Then we'll wait together.

401
00:12:25,419 --> 00:12:28,569
[Ito, eyes on the east. He taps the transmitter three times.]

402
00:12:30,419 --> 00:12:33,569
[Space. The shell’s slats turn, all together, edge-on. Light pours out between them.]

403
00:12:34,619 --> 00:12:36,899
[Low in the east, the Sun is simply there. Gold light floods across the snow and their faces.]

404
00:12:34,719 --> 00:12:35,979
MIRA: There it is!

405
00:12:37,049 --> 00:12:40,199
[Earth from space: the whole day side lights up at once.]

406
00:12:37,149 --> 00:12:40,459
NARRATOR: Everywhere it was day, the Sun came back at once.

407
00:12:40,729 --> 00:12:42,779
[Sydney, 3:52 PM: the surfer on the frozen beach, in the sun again.]

408
00:12:42,929 --> 00:12:44,779
[Tamil Nadu: the farmer in the snow, arms up.]

409
00:12:44,929 --> 00:12:46,779
[Manila: the boy at the bus stop, laughing in the light.]

410
00:12:46,929 --> 00:12:50,079
[The family in the sunlight. Mira holds her drawing up beside the real Sun.]

411
00:12:47,029 --> 00:12:51,364
NARRATOR: At 6:52, the same minute it went out, the Sun came back.

412
00:12:52,634 --> 00:12:55,784
[A memory, warm as home movie: the same roof, every dawn for forty-three years. Mr. Ito, young, then greying, then old, taps his telescope three times as the Sun comes up.]

413
00:12:52,734 --> 00:12:57,619
NARRATOR: Every morning for forty-three years, Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

414
00:12:58,489 --> 00:13:01,639
[Close on his face in the early light.]

415
00:12:58,589 --> 00:13:01,624
NARRATOR: I used to think it was because he liked it.

416
00:13:02,694 --> 00:13:05,844
[Wider: the old man alone on the roof at dawn, tapping the telescope three times.]

417
00:13:02,794 --> 00:13:04,884
NARRATOR: He was waiting for an answer.

418
00:13:06,354 --> 00:13:09,504
[Far out in the dark, a thin thread of light leaves our Sun for one faint star. Somewhere in it, a crayon sun.]

419
00:13:06,454 --> 00:13:09,364
NARRATOR: In twenty-one years, they'll get my drawing.

420
00:13:10,114 --> 00:13:11,879
NARRATOR: I wonder what they'll say.

